From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 23:55:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: rate limit the repeated handover
 message

q6v5_handover_interrupt() is already right to ignore a handover it has
already seen: there is nothing left to do. What it should not do is say
so on every single one, because at least one remote signals it
continuously rather than once.

On SM6375 (Motorola Moto G82 5G) the ADSP re-asserts its handover smp2p
bit roughly 4.2 times a second, from ~30 ms after it boots until it goes
down, on every boot:

	qcom_q6v5_pas a400000.remoteproc: Handover signaled, but it already happened

26355 of them in one 1h44 boot. The interrupt is registered
IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING, so each is a genuine 0->1 edge and the driver is
seeing exactly what the remote is doing; smp2p and the handover logic
are both behaving. The damage is entirely to the log: at that rate the
kernel ring buffer wraps about every six minutes, so dmesg contains
nothing older than that and every message from boot is long gone. On a
device with no serial console that is the difference between being able
to diagnose a driver and not.

Give the message a per-remote ratelimit_state of one message per minute.
__ratelimit() still emits "N callbacks suppressed" on each release, so
the rate remains visible and nothing is hidden -- what goes away is the
flood. Per remote rather than file-static so that one chatty remote
cannot mask another's message.

This is a mitigation and not a fix: why that remote keeps re-signalling
is unknown and unaffected by this patch.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,24 @@
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * Rate limited because at least one remote does this
+		 * continuously rather than once. On SM6375 the ADSP re-asserts
+		 * its handover bit about 4 times a second, for as long as it is
+		 * up: 26355 of these in a single 1h44 boot, which wraps the
+		 * kernel ring buffer roughly every six minutes and takes every
+		 * earlier message with it. Ignoring the interrupt is already
+		 * correct -- the handover has happened and there is nothing
+		 * left to do -- so the only harm was the log volume.
+		 *
+		 * __ratelimit() still reports "N callbacks suppressed" on each
+		 * release, so the rate stays visible and nothing is hidden;
+		 * what goes away is dmesg being useless for anything older than
+		 * a few minutes. Why the remote keeps re-signalling is a
+		 * separate and still open question.
+		 */
+		if (__ratelimit(&q6v5->handover_ratelimit))
+			dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
@@ -351,6 +368,8 @@
 		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, PTR_ERR(q6v5->path),
 				     "failed to acquire interconnect path\n");
 
+	ratelimit_state_init(&q6v5->handover_ratelimit, 60 * HZ, 1);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_q6v5_init);
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.h
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.h
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/completion.h>
+#include <linux/ratelimit.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/qcom_aoss.h>
 
 struct icc_path;
@@ -30,6 +31,7 @@
 	int stop_irq;
 
 	bool handover_issued;
+	struct ratelimit_state handover_ratelimit;
 
 	struct completion start_done;
 	struct completion stop_done;
